          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetic field
            may be decomposed, over a choice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinate+chart"} coordinate chart
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electric+field"} electric field
            and the
            = succeed "." do
              %strong magnetic field
          %p
            See there
            %a{:href => "electromagnetic+field#FieldStrengthAsClosed2Form"} there
            for more.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %div
            %h3#MagneticFluxQuantizationInTypeIISuperconductors Magnetic flux quantization in type II superconductors
            %p
              Due to the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Meissner-Ochsenfeld+effect"} Meissner-Ochsenfeld effect
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superconductor"} superconductor
              placed in a sufficiently small external
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+field"} magnetic field
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi H
                      %mi ext
                    %mo &lt;
                    %msub
                      %mi H
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi c
                          %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_{ext} \lt H_{c_1}
              (aligned along some axis) “expels” that field , making the total magnetic field in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bulk"} bulk
              of the superconductor vanish. However, as the ambient magnetic field exceeds a critical value
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi H
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi c
                            %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_{c_1}
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  for
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+I+superconductors"} type I superconductors
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi H
                          %mi ext
                        %mo &gt;
                        %msub
                          %mi H
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi c
                              %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_{ext} \gt H_{c_1}
                  and the ambient magnetic field fully penetrates the material as for any normal conductor;
              %li
                %p
                  for
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+superconductors"} type II superconductors
                  the superconducting state eventually also breaks down as
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi H
                            %mi ext
                          %mo &gt;
                          %msub
                            %mi H
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi c
                                %mn 2
                          %mo &gt;
                          %msub
                            %mi H
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi c
                                %mn 1
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_{ext} \gt H_{c_2} \gt H_{c_1}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi H
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi c
                              %mn 1
                        %mo &lt;
                        %msub
                          %mi H
                          %mi ext
                        %mo &lt;
                        %msub
                          %mi H
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi c
                              %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_{c_1} \lt H_{ext} \lt H_{c_2}
                  where both regimes mix:
            %div{:style => "margin: -40px 0px 20px 10px"}
              %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
                %img{:src => "/nlab/files/SuperconductorsPhaseDiagram.jpg", :width => "570px"}/
                %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                  From
                  %a{:href => "superconductivity#Chapman00"} Chapman 09
            %p#InThisMixedRegime
              In this mixed regime, a
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+number"} finite number
              of elementary units of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+flux"} magnetic flux
              enter the superconductor, carried by little
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+tubes"} flux tubes
              inside
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vortices"} vortices
              of electric currents:
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vortex+strings"} vortex strings
              (about a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/micron"} micron
              in diameter, e.g.
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "superconductivity#Chapman00"} Chapman 00, p. 559
              %em fluxon
              – while at some small finite distance away from all vortices, the bulk magnetic flux in the superconductor still vanishes (mathematically: it
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vanishes+at+infinity"} vanishes at infinity
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "superconductivity#Chapman00"} Chapman 2009 (2.31)
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "superconductivity#Timm20"} Timm 2020 (6.101)
            #eq:VortexStructure.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
              %code.maruku-mathml
            %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 10px"}
              %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
                %img{:src => "/nlab/files/SuperconductorVortexStructure-230304.jpg", :width => "650px"}/
                %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                  adapted from
                  %a{:href => "superconductivity#LoudonMidgley09"} Loudon and Midgley 2009
            %p
              At sufficiently large density these vortices arrange into hexagonal patterns, first described by
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "superconductivity#Abrikosov57"} Abrikosov 57
              = succeed "." do
                %em Abrikosov vortices
            #eq:HexagonalStructure.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (2)
              %code.maruku-mathml
            %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 10px"}
              %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
                %img{:src => "/nlab/files/AbrikosovVorticesHorizontalSchematics.png", :width => "250px"}/
                %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                  from
                  %a{:href => "#Yu2012"} Yu 2012
            %p
              This flux quantization in type II superconductors is traditionally explained via the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+field+theory"} effective field theory
              provided by the
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Landau-Ginzburg+model"} Landau-Ginzburg model
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "superconductivity#Chapman00"} Chapman 00 (Section 2, culminating in (2.33))
            %p
              But, as indicated a little more explicitly in
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "superconductivity#AlvarezGaume98"} Alvarez-Gaumé 98 (Section IV.B, culminating below IV.11)
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetic field
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/instantons+in+QCD"} instantons in QCD
              in 4d (see also at
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "Yang-Mills+instanton#FromTheMathsToThePhysicsStory"} SU(2)-Instantons – From the correct maths to the traditional physics story
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+charge+quantization"} Dirac charge quantization
              of
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+monopoles"} magnetic monopoles
            %p
              Namely, the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetic field
              is a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+circle+bundle"} connection on a circle bundle
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brown+representability+theorem"} cohomology classifying space
              for the topological class of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetic field
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+space"} classifying space
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BU%28n%29"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \mathrm{U}(1)
              of the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+group"} circle group
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-MacLane+space"} Eilenberg-MacLane space
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(\mathbb{Z},2)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+group"} homotopy group
              the
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integers"} integers
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ≃
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} {
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 2
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} }
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} /
                        %mi hmpty
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ≃
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\pi_2(B \mathrm{U}(1))
                    \\;\simeq\;
                    \\big\{  S^2 \to B \mathrm{U}(1)\big\}_{\big/hmpty}
                    \\;\simeq\;
                    \\mathbb{Z}
                    \\,.
            %p
              This implies that on every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
              which looks, up to
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equivalence"} homotopy equivalence
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-sphere"} 2-sphere
              to the electromagnetic field, the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+flux"} magnetic flux
              is identified with an element in the group of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integers"} integers
              %em quantized
              in integral multiples of some
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physical+unit"} unit
              flux.
            %p
              For the case of a (hypothetical)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+monopole"} magnetic monopole
              (e.g. a magnetically
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/charged+black+hole"} charged black hole
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
              %em around
              the monopole (the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complement"} complement
              of its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline"} worldline
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %mn 0
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{0,1}
              in the ambient (asymtptotically)
